/*
Problem: Sum of Squares of Special Elements (LC 2778)
Topic: Array / Math

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

An element is called special if its
1-based index divides the length of
the array.

For every such element, add the square
of its value to the answer.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Let n be the size of the array.

2. Traverse the array.

3. Convert the 0-based index to
   1-based by using (i + 1).

4. If:

   n % (i + 1) == 0

   then this is a special element.

5. Add:

   nums[i] × nums[i]

   to the answer.

6. Return the final sum.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

nums = [1,2,3,4]

n = 4

Special indices:

1, 2, 4

Elements:

1, 2, 4

Answer:

1² + 2² + 4²

= 1 + 4 + 16

= 21

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

nums = [2,7,1,19,18,3]

n = 6

Special indices:

1, 2, 3, 6

Elements:

2, 7, 1, 3

Answer:

4 + 49 + 1 + 9

= 63

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

The condition:

n % (i + 1) == 0

checks whether the 1-based index is a
divisor of the array length.

Only those elements contribute to the
final answer.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

One traversal of the array.

Space : O(1)

Only the answer variable is used.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Array Traversal / Math
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int sumOfSquares(vector<int>& nums) {

        int n = nums.size();
        int sumOfSquares = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            // Check if (i + 1) divides n
            if (n % (i + 1) == 0) {

                sumOfSquares += nums[i] * nums[i];
            }
        }

        return sumOfSquares;
    }
};
